<ProseLayout
  title="You learned how to build. Nobody taught you the business."
  description="Developer Business Lab makes practical business systems for freelance developers who want their technical skills to produce more income, more control, and a business that works for the long run."
  eyebrow="About Developer Business Lab"
>
  <p>Developer Business Lab makes practical business systems for freelance developers: how to define what you sell, price it, find clients, close deals, and keep them. So your skills earn what they should, and freelancing gives you the freedom you went independent for.</p>

  <h2>Your skills are already making someone money.</h2>

  <p>When you worked for an agency or a company, your code was worth far more than your paycheck. Someone else captured the difference, because they knew how to turn development work into a business.</p>

  <p>Most developers go independent to capture that value themselves: more income, control over their time, no boss, and the freedom to do the work right. Then they find out nobody ever taught them the other half. How to price a project. Where the next client comes from. What to say on a sales call. How to keep a client after launch.</p>

  <p>So they figure it out alone, through scattered advice and expensive mistakes. Developer Business Lab exists to close that gap.</p>

  <h2>What we believe</h2>

  <p><strong>Your skills aren't the problem.</strong></p>
  <p>Most freelance developers who struggle are good at the work. What's missing is the business built around it.</p>

  <p><strong>Business is a system, not a personality.</strong></p>
  <p>You don't need to become a salesman or an influencer. Pricing, finding clients, and closing deals are processes with inputs, steps, and results you can measure. If you can learn a framework, you can learn this.</p>

  <p><strong>The parts are connected.</strong></p>
  <p>A vague offer makes pricing hard. Weak pricing makes every deal feel desperate. An empty pipeline makes you take bad clients. That's why isolated tips rarely stick, and why every system here is built to fit with the others.</p>

  <p><strong>Evidence beats advice.</strong></p>
  <p>The fastest way to learn what works is to try it with real buyers and look at what happens. Every system is built around doing the work and learning from the results, not reading about it.</p>

  <p><strong>Start with the smallest thing that helps.</strong></p>
  <p>You shouldn't have to buy everything to fix one problem. If one thing is broken, fix that. If several things are, build the foundation. There's no required order and no pressure to buy more than you need.</p>

  <h2>Built to be used, not just read.</h2>

  <p>Every system pairs a guide with fill-in workbooks. The guide explains the thinking behind each decision in plain language. The workbook turns that thinking into something you complete with your own clients, projects, and numbers: a defined offer, a pricing decision, a pipeline, a sales plan, or a retainer.</p>

  <p>Worked examples show how a decision gets made, step by step. By the end, you haven't just learned a framework. You've built a working piece of your business, and you know how to make the next decision yourself.</p>

  <p>And everything is written for people who sell development work: scope creep on builds, competing with Upwork and page builders, subcontracting for agencies, and turning finished sites into ongoing income.</p>

  <h2>Judge the work by the work</h2>

  <p>You don't have to take our word for it.</p>

  <p>Every product page shows what the system covers, how it works, and a look inside the workbooks, so you can see whether it matches your problem before you buy.</p>

  <p>Want to try the approach first? The free <a href="/free/">Developer Marketing Quickstart</a> takes you through one real round of finding clients.</p>

  <p>And every purchase is covered by a <a href="/refund-policy/">14-Day Satisfaction Guarantee</a>.</p>

